# Git commits — no AI-tool attribution

When creating commits (only when the user explicitly asks), **never** attribute authorship to Cursor, Claude, OpenCode, or any AI tool in git metadata.

## Author identity

- Use the repo's existing git config (`user.name`, `user.email`) — **do not** change git config.
- **Never** pass `--author`, `GIT_AUTHOR_NAME`, `GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL`, `GIT_COMMITTER_NAME`, or `GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL` to impersonate an AI tool.
- **Never** add `Co-authored-by:` trailers for Cursor, Claude, Composer, Copilot, or similar.

## Commit message & description

- **Never** mention Cursor, Claude, Composer, AI, or "generated by" in the subject, body, or PR description you draft.
- Write commit messages as the human developer would: what changed and why, in conventional-commit style once the repo adopts one (e.g. `feat: add appointment exclusion constraint`).

## Examples

```text
❌ Co-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
❌ feat: add patient search (via Cursor)

✅ feat: add fuzzy patient search by name/contact/ID
✅ fix: prevent double-booking under concurrent requests
```
